      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-242680",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/6b/6bab9c4a2a9b6cc12d70462aaa6dd9151de61e2eee1663c8728aa306da38325e.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $L$ and $M$ are the midpoints of $BC^(\u2015)$ and $AB^(\u2015)$, respectively. You may assume without proof that\n$\\frac{BA}{BM} = 2$.\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> $AC = 2LM$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of this statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $L$ is the midpoint of $BC^(\u2015)$ | Given |\n| 2 | $BC = 2BL$ | {{field-1}} |\n| 3 | $\\frac{BC}{BL} = 2$ | Multiplication principle |\n| 4 | $\\frac{BA}{BM} = 2$ | Given |\n| 5 | $\\frac{BA}{BM} = \\frac{BC}{BL}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $\u2220ABC\u2245\u2220MBL$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 7 | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| 8 | $\\frac{AC}{LM} = \\frac{BC}{BL}$ | {{field-4}} |\n| 9 | $\\frac{AC}{LM} = 2$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 10 | $AC = 2LM$ | Multiplication principle |"
    },
