      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-242752",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/0c/0cd6a45247e72657d6ab74236b32abb0b9d962617d0746cbbfb988c062993e47.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $C_{1}$ and $B_{1}$ are the midpoints of $AB^(\u2015)$ and $CA^(\u2015)$, respectively. You may assume without proof that\n$\\frac{AC}{AB_{1}} = 2, \\frac{AB}{AC_{1}} = 2$.\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> $CB = 2C_{1}B_{1}$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of this statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| 2 | $\\frac{AC}{AB_{1}} = 2$ | Given |\n| 3 | $\\frac{AB}{AC_{1}} = 2$ | Given |\n| 4 | $\\frac{AC}{AB_{1}} = \\frac{AB}{AC_{1}}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 5 | $\u25b3CAB\u223c\u25b3B_{1}AC_{1}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 6 | $\\frac{CB}{C_{1}B_{1}} = \\frac{AB}{AC_{1}}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| 7 | $\\frac{CB}{C_{1}B_{1}} = 2$ | {{field-4}} |\n| 8 | $CB = 2C_{1}B_{1}$ | Multiplication principle |"
    }
